# tools/sudoers.d/claude-codex-audit — T-1884
#
# SOURCE COPY for review; the operator installs to /etc/sudoers.d/claude-codex-audit.
# Grants claude exactly one NOPASSWD command as codex: the INSTALLED wrapper
# at /usr/local/bin/pantheon-s3star-tmux (root:root, mode 0755) — see the
# paired tools/sudoers.d/pantheon-s3star-tmux for what it validates/execs.
#
# Why one fixed-PATH line instead of tmux-command Cmnd_Aliases: an earlier
# draft tried scoping this directly to the three tmux command shapes
# (`bash -lc "tmux new-session ..."` etc.) via wildcard Cmnd_Aliases. That
# shape has no way to verify pre-install (sudoers' fnmatch-over-a-quoted-
# string matching semantics can only be confirmed against a live install),
# and a wildcard mismatch discovered post-install would put us right back
# at password prompts. Pointing the grant at a single fixed, root-owned
# executable instead means every real constraint (verb, session-name shape,
# workspace-path prefix) lives in that script's bash validation, which is
# plain-unittest-testable pre-install — see
# tools/test_s3star_audit_runner.py's WrapperScriptTests. This is P1
# (architecture over policy): the sudoers line itself needs no runtime
# verification beyond "does it match this one path."
#
# THREAT MODEL: this grants claude -> codex COMMAND EXECUTION via the
# wrapper only — NOT root, and NOT arbitrary commands as codex. It does
# NOT grant `sudo -u codex bash` or `sudo -u codex <anything else>` — only
# this one fixed path, which itself only execs `tmux new-session|send-keys|
# kill-session` against sessions shaped like the runner's own
# `codex_audit_session_name()` output (see the wrapper's SESSION_RE).
#
# The wrapper also carries test-only env overrides (S3STAR_WORKSPACE_ROOT,
# S3STAR_TMUX_BIN) for its own pre-install unit tests. Their inertness
# under this grant is CODE-ENFORCED in the wrapper (it unsets both whenever
# SUDO_USER is set — sudo always sets that on the process it execs), not
# merely a side effect of this grant omitting SETENV/env_keep for them —
# see the wrapper's header for the full reasoning (T-1884 REBOUND).
#
# CRITICAL: the Cmnd path below MUST stay /usr/local/bin/pantheon-s3star-tmux
# (the installed, root-owned copy) — NEVER a path under this repo.
# pantheon-config is claude-writable; a NOPASSWD grant pointing at a
# claude-writable path would let a compromised claude session rewrite the
# target and run arbitrary code as codex, defeating the wrapper entirely.
#
# INSTALL (the operator):
#   visudo -cf tools/sudoers.d/claude-codex-audit
#   sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 tools/sudoers.d/pantheon-s3star-tmux /usr/local/bin/pantheon-s3star-tmux
#   sudo install -o root -g root -m 0440 tools/sudoers.d/claude-codex-audit /etc/sudoers.d/claude-codex-audit
#
# VERIFY (the operator, post-install — this is the real proof; visudo -c only
# checks syntax, not runtime matching):
#   sudo -n -l -U claude
#     # confirm the grant is listed
#   sudo -n -u codex /usr/local/bin/pantheon-s3star-tmux kill codex-audit-weekly-narrow-1900-01-01
#     # expect a clean tmux "can't find session" failure (exit nonzero, NOT
#     # a password prompt / hang) — proves the grant actually matches.

claude ALL=(codex) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/pantheon-s3star-tmux
